3. ONE TEST READ CLOSELY: framebuffer::selftest()'s 24-bpp guard byte
================================================================================

    let mut buf24 = [0x55u8; 8];
    let fb24 = Framebuffer { address: buf24.as_mut_ptr(), width: 1, height: 1,
                             pitch: 3, bpp: 24, memory_model: 1, ... };
    fb24.pixel(0, 0, &Rgb::new(0x12, 0x34, 0x56));
    if buf24[0] != 0x56 || buf24[1] != 0x34 || buf24[2] != 0x12 { ok = false; }
    if buf24[3] != 0x55 { ok = false; }        // the W1 #4 invariant

  Left: the eight bytes of an array the test allocated and filled with 0x55
  itself. Right: three bytes of expected packed colour and one untouched sentinel.
  Where they came from: the sentinel is the test's own choice; the colour bytes
  are the little-endian packing of 0x123456, which is a format fact, not a value
  read back from pixel(). So the check is not circular: it does not ask pixel()
  what it wrote, it looks at memory next to the pixel. If pixel() writes a u32 at
  24 bpp, byte 3 becomes 0x00 and the check fails — which is exactly the defect
  the work order named, and my plant D shows this suite does go red when a
  framebuffer defect is reintroduced.

